"To compute the product of the fractions, we can simplify the expression by canceling out common factors in the numerator and the denominator.\n\nFirst, let's write out the product:\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{6}{4} \\cdot \\frac{8}{5} \\cdot \\frac{10}{6} \\cdot \\frac{12}{7} \\cdot \\frac{14}{8}\n\\]\n\nWe can group the numerators together and the denominators together:\n\\[\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 14}{3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 8}\n\\]\n\nNext, we can cancel out the common factors in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 7}{3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2} \\frac{7}{1} = 7\n\\]\n\nThus, the product is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]"
