"For the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + mx + (m+n) = 0\\) to have one real solution, its discriminant must be zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\).\n\nIn this case, \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = m\\), and \\(c = m + n\\). Therefore, the discriminant is:\n\\[\n\\Delta = m^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (m + n) = m^2 - 4m - 4n = 0\n\\]\n\nSince the discriminant must be zero for the quadratic equation to have one real solution, we have:\n\\[\nm^2 - 4m - 4n = 0\n\\]\n\nThis equation can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n(m - 2)^2 = 4n\n\\]\n\nFor this equation to hold true, \\(m - 2\\) must be zero. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nn = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(n\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\)."
