"To determine the value of \\( c \\) for which the quadratic equation \\( 3x^2 + 4x + c = 0 \\) has exactly one solution, we need to ensure that the discriminant of the quadratic equation is zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nFor the given equation \\( 3x^2 + 4x + c = 0 \\), we have \\( a = 3 \\), \\( b = 4 \\), and \\( c = c \\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 4^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot c = 16 - 12c\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have exactly one solution, the discriminant must be zero:\n\n\\[\n16 - 12c = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( c \\):\n\n\\[\n16 = 12c \\implies c = 1\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer:\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{1}\\)."
