"Let $c$ be the number of chocolate candies, $v$ be the number of vanilla candies, and $p$ be the number of peppermint candies. Let $l$ be the number of lemon candies. We have the following equations:\n\n1. $c + v + p + l = 15$\n2. $p + l = 2(c + v)$\n3. $p = l + 8$\n\nSubstitute $p = l + 8$ into the first equation:\n\n$$c + v + (l + 8) + l = 15 \\Rightarrow c + v + 2l + 8 = 15$$\n\nNow, substitute $c + v = 2l + 8$ into the second equation:\n\n$$l + 8 + l = 2(2l + 8) \\Rightarrow 2l + 8 = 4l + 16 \\Rightarrow 8 - 16 = 2l - l \\Rightarrow l = 4$$\n\nSo, the number of lemon candies is $\\boxed{4}$.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $4$. I hope it is correct."
"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{3x^2-4x+1}{x-1}=m\\), we first simplify the left-hand side. We can use polynomial division to simplify \\(\\frac{3x^2-4x+1}{x-1}\\).\n\nPerforming the division:\n\n\\[\n3x^2 - 4x + 1 \\div (x-1) = 3x - 1 + \\frac{2}{x-1}\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n3x - 1 + \\frac{2}{x-1} = m\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x \\neq 1\\), the term \\(\\frac{2}{x-1}\\) is always positive and can take any positive value. Therefore, \\(m\\) can take any positive value.\n\nTo find the values that \\(m\\) cannot have, we note that \\(m\\) cannot be negative because the term \\(\\frac{2}{x-1}\\) is always positive and cannot be negative.\n\nThus, the set of real values that \\(m\\) cannot have is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, \\infty)}\n\\]"
