"To solve the expression \\(8[6^2 - 3(11)]\\div 8 + 3\\), we need to follow the order of operations (PEMDAS/BODMAS), which stands for Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication and Division (from left to right), Addition and Subtraction (from left to right).\n\n1. **Exponents:**\n   \\[\n   6^2 = 36\n   \\]\n\n2. **Parentheses:**\n   \\[\n   36 - 3 \\cdot 11 = 36 - 33 = 3\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   8 \\cdot 3 = 24\n   \\]\n\n4. **Division:**\n   \\[\n   24 \\div 8 = 3\n   \\]\n\n5. **Addition:**\n   \\[\n   3 + 3 = 6\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).\n\nI hope it is correct. \n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{6}\\)."
"We start with the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n5x - 2y &= 7, \\\\\n3x + y &= 2.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we solve the second equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[\ny = 2 - 3x.\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( y \\) in the first equation:\n\\[\n5x - 2(2 - 3x) = 7.\n\\]\n\nSimplify and solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n5x - 4 + 6x = 7 \\implies 11x = 11 \\implies x = 1.\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( x = 1 \\) back into the expression for \\( y \\):\n\\[\ny = 2 - 3(1) = -1.\n\\]\n\nFinally, we compute \\( 24x - 14y \\):\n\\[\n24(1) - 14(-1) = 24 + 14 = \\boxed{38}.\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer:\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{38}\\)."
