"Let the two numbers be \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). We are given two equations:\n\n1. \\( x + y = 3 \\)\n2. \\( x^2 - y^2 = 33 \\)\n\nWe need to find the absolute value of the difference of the two numbers, which is \\( |x - y| \\).\n\nFirst, we use the identity for the difference of squares:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y)\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the given values into the identity:\n\\[\n33 = 3(x - y)\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\( x - y \\):\n\\[\nx - y = \\frac{33}{3} = 11\n\\]\n\nThus, the absolute value of the difference of the two numbers is:\n\\[\n|x - y| = 11\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{11}\\)."
