"To determine the number of distinguishable ways to write $9$ as the sum of $1\\text{'s},$2\\text{'s},$ and $4\\text{'s}$ where the order of the addends matters, we can use the concept of partitions of a number into distinct parts.\n\nThe number of ways to partition a number \\( n \\) into distinct parts of size \\( k \\) is given by the formula:\n\\[ \\binom{n+k-1}{k-1} \\]\n\nIn this problem, \\( n = 9 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\). Therefore, we need to calculate:\n\\[ \\binom{9+3-1}{3-1} = \\binom{11}{2} \\]\n\nThe binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{11}{2}\\) is calculated as follows:\n\\[ \\binom{11}{2} = \\frac{11 \\times 10}{2 \\times 1} = 55 \\]\n\nThus, the number of distinguishable ways to write $9$ as the sum of $1\\text{'s},$ $2\\text{'s},$ and $\\text\\text{'s}$ is \\(\\boxed{55}\\)."
