"To determine the number of ways to put 4 balls into 3 distinguishable boxes, where two balls are indistinguishable green and two are indistinguishable red, we can use the concept of permutations with repetition.\n\nFirst, consider the total number of ways to place 4 balls into 3 distinguishable boxes if all balls were distinguishable. Each ball has 3 choices, so the total number of ways is:\n\n\\[ 3^4 = 81 \\]\n\nHowever, we need to account for the indistinguishability of the balls. Since there are 2 indistinguishable green balls and 2 indistinguishable red balls, we must divide by the number of ways to arrange these pairs.\n\nThe number of ways to arrange 2 indistinguishable green balls and 2 indistinguishable red balls is:\n\n\\[ 2! \\times 2! = 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\]\n\nTherefore, we divide the total number of distinguishable arrangements by the number of ways to arrange the indistinguishable pairs:\n\n\\[ \\frac{81}{4} = 27 \\]\n\nThus, the number of ways to put 4 balls into 3 distinguishable boxes is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{27} \\]"
