"To find the probability that Larry Ponds will earn a walk exactly once in his next two plate appearances, we need to consider the sequence of events: he earns a walk on the first plate appearance and does not earn a walk on the second plate appearance.\n\nThe probability of earning a walk on a single plate appearance is \\(\\frac{2}{5}\\), and the probability of not earning a walk on a single plate appearance is \\(1 - \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{3}{5}\\).\n\nThe probability of earning a walk on the first plate appearance is \\(\\frac{2}{5}\\).\nThe probability of not earning a walk on the second plate appearance is \\(\\frac{3}{5}\\).\n\nThe combined probability of these two events happening is the product of their individual probabilities:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) = \\frac{2}{5} \\times \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{6}{25}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Larry Ponds will earn a walk exactly onceFinal Answer next two plate appearances is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{6}{25}}\\)."
"To compute $\\binom{11}{4}$, we use the formula for combinations:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\n\nHere, $n = 11$ and $r = 4$. Plugging in these values, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{11}{4} = \\frac{11!}{4!(11-4)!} = \\frac{11!}{4! \\cdot 7!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the common terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{11}{4} = \\frac{11 \\times 10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{4! \\times 7!} = \\frac{11 \\times 10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{990}{24} =  = 330\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $\\binom{11}{4}$ is $\\boxed{330}$."
