"To determine the number of ways Mr. Cole can select 3 girls from 6 and 5 boys from 11, we need to calculate the number of combinations for each group and then multiply them together.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of ways to choose 3 girls from 6:\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{3} = \\frac{6!}{3!(6-3)!} = \\frac{6!}{3!3!} = 20\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 5 boys from 11:\n\\[\n\\binom{11}{5} = \\frac{11!}{5!(11-5)!} = \\frac{11!}{5!6!} = 462\n\\]\n\nNow, we multiply these two results together to find the total number of ways to select 3 girls and 5 boys:\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{3} \\times \\binom{11}{5} = 20 \\times 462 = 9240\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{9240}\\) different ways."
"To find the probability that the dart will not land on the target, we can use the fact that the sum probability of all possible outcomes must equal 1. In this case, the two possible outcomes are the dart landing on the target or the dart not landing on the target.\n\nGiven:\n- The probability that the dart will land on the target is \\(\\frac{3}{8}\\).\n\nLet \\(P(\\text{not target})\\) be the probability that the dart will not land on the target.\n\nSince the sum of the probabilities of all possible outcomes is 1, we have:\n\\[ P(\\text{target}) + P(\\text{not target}) = 1 \\]\n\nSubstituting the given probability:\n\\[ \\frac{3}{8} + P(\\text{not target}) = 1 \\]\n\nSolving for \\(P(\\text{not target})\\):\n\\[ P(\\text{not target}) = 1 - \\frac{3}{8} = \\frac{8}{8} - \\frac{3}{8} = \\frac{5}{8} \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{8}}\\)."
