"To determine how many times the digit 8 appears in the list of all integers from 1 to 1000, we can break down the problem into counting the occurrences of the digit 8 in the tens place and the units place.\n\nFirst, consider the tens place:\n- The digit 8 appears in the tens place once every 100 numbers.\n- Therefore, in 1000 numbers, the digit 8 appears in the tens place \\(\\frac{1000}{100} = 10\\) times.\n\nNext, consider the units place:\n- The digit 8 appears in the units place once every 10 numbers.\n- Therefore, in 1000 numbers, the digit 8 appears in the units place \\(\\frac{1000}{10} = 100\\) times.\n\nAdding these two results together, we get:\n\\[ 10 \\text{ (tens in the tens place)} + 100 \\text{ (times in the units place)} = \\boxed{110} \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{110}\\)."
"To determine the probability that both cubes will land with one red face and one white face up, we need to consider the possible outcomes for each cube and then combine the probabilities.\n\nEach cube has 6 faces, with 3 red faces and 3 white faces. Therefore, the probability of rolling a red face on a single cube is \\(\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\\), and the probability of rolling a white face on a single cube is also \\(\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nFor both cubes to land with one red face and one white face up, one cube must show a red face and the other must show a white face. The probability of this happening is the product of the probabilities of each individual event:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{one red and one white}) = P(\\text{one red}) \\times P(\\text{one white}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that the two cubes will land with one red face and one white face up is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\\)."
