"To find the measure of angle \\( AOB \\) in the circle with center \\( O \\) and diameters \\( AC \\) and \\( BD \\), we start by noting that angle \\( AOD \\) is a central angle subtending the arc \\( AD \\). The measure of angle \\( AOD \\) is given as \\( 54 \\) degrees.\n\nSince \\( AC \\) and \\( BD \\) are diameters of the circle, angle \\( AOC \\) is a right angle (90 degrees), and angle \\( BOD \\) is also a angle angle (90 degrees). Therefore, angle \\( AOB \\) is a central angle subtending the same arc \\( AD \\) as angle \\( AOD \\).\n\nThe measure of a central angle is half the measure of the arc it subtends. Therefore, the measure of angle \\( AOB \\) is half the measure of the arc \\( AD \\). Since the measure of angle \\( AOD \\) is \\( 54 \\) degrees, the measure of angle \\( AOB \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\times 54 degrees = 27 \\text{ degrees}\n\\]\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\( AOB \\) is \\(\\boxed{27}\\)."
"Let \\( n \\) be the number of sides of the polygon. The measure of an interior angle of a regular polygon is given by \\(\\frac{(n-2) \\cdot 180^\\circ}{n}\\), and the measure of an exterior angle is \\(\\frac{90^\\circ}{n}\\). According to the problem, the measure of an interior angle is 6.5 times the measure of an exterior angle. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2) \\cdot 180^\\circ}{n} = 6.5 \\cdot \\frac{90^\\circ}{n}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n(n-2) \\cdot 180^\\circ = 585^\\circ\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by \\(180^\\circ\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nn-2 = 3.5\n\\]\n\nAdding 2 to both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\nn = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the polygon has \\(\\boxed{6}\\) sides.\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $6$. I hope it is correct."
