"To solve the equation \\( x^5 = x^4 + 72x^3 \\), we start by rearranging all terms to one side of the equation:\n\n\\[ x^5 - x^4 - 72x^3 = 0 \\]\n\nNext, we factor out the greatest common factor \\( x^3 \\):\n\n\\[ x^3(x^2 - 1 - 72) = 0 \\]\n\nThis gives us two factors to consider:\n\n1. \\( x^3 = 0 \\)\n2. \\( x^2 - 73 = 0 \\)\n\nFor the first factor, \\( x^3 = 0 \\), we have:\n\n\\[ x = 0 \\]\n\nFor the second factor, \\( x^2 - 73 = 0 \\), we have:\n\n\\[ x^2 = 73 \\]\n\nSince \\( x^2 \\) cannot be negative, there are no real solutions for this factor. Therefore, the only real solution is:\n\n\\[ x = 0 \\]\n\nThus, the number of different values of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\)."
