"Since $x^6 + ax + b$ is divisible by $x^2 - 2x - 1,$ we can write\n\n\\[x^6 + ax + b = (x^2 - 2x - 1)(x^4 + cx + d).\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get\n\n\\[x^6 + ax + b = x^6 + cx^5 - 2cx^5 - 2cx^4 - x^4 - cx - d.\\]\n\nEquating coefficients, we have\n\n\\[a = c - 2c - 1 = -c - 1,\\]\n\\[b = -d.\\]\n\nSince $x^6 + ax + b$ is divisible by $x^2 - 2x - 1,$ we know that $c = 0$ and $d = -1.$ Therefore,\n\n\\[a = -0 - 1 = -1,\\]\n\\[b = 1.\\]\n\nThus, $a + b = (-1) + 1 = \\boxed{2}.$\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $2$. I hope it is correct."
