"We start by simplifying the given expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)(1 - xy)}{(1 + x^2)(1 + y^2)}.\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's expand the numerator:\n\\[\n(x + y)(1 - xy) = x + y - xy - x^2y - xy^2 = 1 - x^2y - xy^2.\n\\]\n\nNext, let's expand the denominator:\n\\[\n(1 + x^2)(1 + y^2) = 1 + x^2 + y^2 + x^2y^2.\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{1 - x^2y - xy^2}{1 + x^2 + y^2 + x^2y^2}.\n\\]\n\nNotice that the numerator and the denominator are identical. Therefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{1} = 1.\n\\]\n\nSince the expression always simplifies to 1 for any \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), the set of possible values of the expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{1\\}}.\n\\]"
