"To determine the units digit of \\(7^{53}\\), we can focus on the units digits of the powers of 7. Let's observe the pattern in the units digits:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n7^1 &= 7 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)} \\\\\n7^2 &= 49 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)} \\\\\n7^3 &= 343 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)} \\\\\n7^4 &= 2401 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)} \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe notice that the units digits repeat every 4 powers: 7, 9, 3, 1. To find the units digit of \\(7^{53}\\), we need to determine the remainder when 53 is divided by 4:\n\n\\[\n53 \\div 4 = 13 \\text{ remainder } 1\n\\]\n\nThe remainder is 1, which corresponds to the units digit of \\(7^3\\) in our pattern. Therefore, the units digit of \\(7^{53}\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\)."
"We need to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of $13n + 8$ and $5n + 3$. Let's denote this gcd by $d$.\n\nFirst, we use the Euclidean algorithm to express $d$ in terms of $n$:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nd &= \\gcd(13n + 8, 5n + 3) \\\\\n&= \\gcd(13n + 8, 5n + 3 - 2(13n + 8)) \\\\\n&= \\gcd(13n + 8, -21n - 13) \\\\\n&= \\gcd(13n + 8, 13)\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSince $13n + 8$ is a multiple of $13$ for any integer $n$, the gcd of $13n + 8$ and $13$ is always $13$. Therefore, the maximum possible value of $13n + 8$ and $5n + 3$ is $\\boxed{13}$.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $13$. I hope it is correct"
