"To find the smallest integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 5n + 3 > -10 \\), we will solve the inequality step by step.\n\n1. Start with the inequality:\n   \\[\n   5n + 3 > -10\n   \\]\n\n2. Subtract 3 from both sides to isolate the term with \\( n \\):\n   \\[\n   5n + 3 - 3 > -10 - 3\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   5n > -13\n   \\]\n\n3. Divide both sides by 5 to solve for \\( n \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5n}{5} > \\frac{-13}{5}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   n > -2.6\n   \\]\n\nSince \\( n \\) must be an integer, the smallest integer greater than \\(-2.6\\) is \\(-2\\). Therefore, the smallest integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 5n + 3 > -10 \\) is \\(\\boxed{-2}\\)."
"To determine how far Bill is from his starting point, we need to calculate the net displacement in both the north and east directions and then use the Pythagorean theorem to find the straight-line distance.\n\nFirst, let's find the net displacement in the north-south direction:\n- Bill walks \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) mile south.\n- Then he walks \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) mile south again.\n- The net displacement in the north-south direction is \\(0\\) miles.\n\nNext, let's find the net displacement in the east-west direction:\n- Bill walks \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) mile east.\n- The net displacement in the east-west direction is \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) mile.\n\nNow, we use the Pythagorean theorem to find the straight-line distance:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{0^2 + \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{0 + \\frac{9}{16}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}} = 0.5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Bill is \\(\\boxed{0.5}\\) miles from his starting point."
