"To compute \\( \\frac{1}{31} \\div \\frac{1}{93} \\), we can use the property of fractions that states dividing by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying by its reciprocal. Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{31} \\div \\frac{1}{93} = \\frac{1}{31} \\times \\frac{93}{1}\n\\]\n\nNext, we perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{31} \\times \\frac{93}{1} = \\frac{1 \\times 93}{31 \\times 1} = \\frac{93}{31}\n\\]\n\nNow, we simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{93}{31}\\). The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 93 and 31 is 31. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by their GCD:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{93 \\div 31}{31 \\div 31} = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]"
