"To find the area of the gray figure, we need to calculate the areas of the three squares and then subtract the areas of the overlapping regions.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of one square:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one square} = 6^2 = 36 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nSince there are three squares, the total area of the three squares is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 36 = 108 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the area of the overlapping regions. Notice that the squares overlap such that each square is covered by two other squares. Therefore, the area of the overlapping regions is equal to the area of one square:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of overlapping regions} = 36 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nNow, we subtract the area of the overlapping regions from the total area of the three squares to find the area of the gray figure:\n\\[\n108 - 36 = 72 \\quad \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of the gray figure is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{72}\n\\]"
