"To determine the tax for a house assessed at \\$160,000, we first need to find the tax rate. The tax rate is the tax paid divided by the assessed value of the house.\n\nGiven:\n- Assessed value of the house = \\$ \\$120,000\n- Tax paid = \\$ \\$3,000\n\nThe tax rate is:\n\\[\n\\text{Tax rate} = \\frac{\\text{Tax paid}}{\\text{Assessed value}} = \\frac{3,000}{120,000} = 0.025\n\\]\n\nNow, we use this tax rate to find the tax for a house assessed at \\$160,000:\n\\[\n\\text{Tax for } \\$160,000 = \\text{Tax rate} \\times \\text{Assessed value} = 0.025 \\times 160,000 = 4,000\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the tax for a house assessed at \\$160,000 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4,000}\n\\]"
"Let \\( x \\) be the number of blue marbles to be added to the bag. Initially, there are 20 marbles, 5 of which are blue. After adding \\( x \\) blue marbles, the total number of marbles will be \\( 20 + x \\), and the number of blue marbles will be \\( 5 + x \\).\n\nThe probability of selecting a blue marble at random is given by the ratio of the number of blue marbles to the total number of marbles. We want this probability to be \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\). Therefore, we set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5 + x}{20 + x} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\), we cross-multiply:\n\n\\[\n2(5 + x) = 20 + x\n\\]\n\n\\[\n10 + 2x = 20 + x\n\\]\n\n\\[\n10 + 2x - 20 = x - x\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = 10\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of blue marbles that must be added to the bag is \\(\\boxed{10}\\)."
