"To find the measure of the largest angle in a triangle, we start by noting that the sum of the interior angles in any triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\). Let the measures of the three angles be \\(x\\), \\(3x\\), and \\(6x\\) respectively, according to the given ratio \\(1:3:6\\).\n\nThe equation for the sum of the angles is:\n\\[\nx + 3x + 6x = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\\[\n10x = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we divide both sides by 10:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180^\\circ}{10} = 18^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find the measures of the angles:\n\\[\n6x = 6 \\times 18^\\circ = 108^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of the largest angle is \\(\\boxed{108^\\circ}\\) degrees.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(108^\\circ\\). I hope it is correct."
"We can use the principle of inclusion-exclusion to solve this problem. Let \\( H \\) be the set of students taking history and \\( S \\) be the set of students taking science. We are given:\n- \\( |H| = 126 \\)\n- \\( |S| = 129 \\)\n- \\( |H \\cup S| = 200 \\)\n\nWe need to find \\( |H \\cap S| \\), the number of students taking both history and science. According to the principle of inclusion-exclusion:\n\\[\n|H \\cup S| = |H| + |S| - |H \\cap S|\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n200 = 126 + 129 - |H \\cap S|\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( |H \\cap S| \\):\n\\[\n200 = 255 - |H \\cap S|\n\\]\n\\[\n|H \\cap S| = 255 - 200 = 55\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students taking both history and science is:\n\\(\\boxed{55}\\)."
