"To find the inverse of the matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to determine if it is invertible. A matrix is invertible if and only if its determinant is non-zero.\n\nThe determinant of a 2x2 matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by \\(ad - bc\\).\n\nFor the given matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), the determinant is:\n\\[\n\\text{det} = (5)(1) - (-4)(0) = 5\n\\]\n\nSince the determinant is non-zero, the matrix is invertible. The inverse of a 2x2 matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by \\(\\frac{1}{\\text{det}} \\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\\\ -c & a \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThus, the inverse of the given matrix is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{5} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]"
