"We have that $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 2$ and $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}.$\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix}.$ Then\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} &= x + y + z = 2, \\\\\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{v} &= \\begin{pmatrix} -y - z \\\\ x - z \\\\ x - y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n-y - z &= 1, \\\\\nx - z &= -2, \\\\\nx - y &= 1.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSolving this system of equations, we find\n\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= 1,\ny &= 0,\nz &= 1.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{v} = \\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}}.$"
